// A dip and a fenced ember, then two trenches at the feet of two cliffs; an echo grows from a stone near each cliff, and the cliff cuts it short.
name Echo
rule B1357/S1357
steps 3
hint A stranger law: a cell lives when its neighbour count is odd. Each echo grew for three generations from somewhere on a trench floor, and the cliff cut off whatever reached it. The echo's top is the only way up each cliff.
tip Three generations reach three cells out. Count three back from the echo's open edge, on the floor, and you have the stone. It is not in the middle of what you can see.
intro Deeper in, the law is stranger. What lived here echoed, and the echoes climbed the cliffs before the rock cut them short. Where did each begin?
outro An echo is the shape of its source, repeated and thinned. You are learning the grammar.
